  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-121800",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={315}^{∘}$ in the $4$th quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
